00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,360
Part 4 You will hear an anthropology student giving

2
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:10,120
a presentation on spiral path designs known as labyrinth.

3
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:13,920
First you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,060
you

5
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:12,840
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

6
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:17,920
Labyrinths have existed for well over 4,000 years.

7
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:22,760
Labyrinths and labyrinthine symbols have been found in regions as diverse as modern-day

8
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:27,480
Turkey, Ireland, Greece and India.

9
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:32,120
There are various designs of labyrinth but what they all have in common is a winding

10
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:36,880
spiral path which leads to a central area.

11
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:43,080
There is one starting point at the entrance and the goal is to reach the central area.

12
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:48,240
Finding your way through a labyrinth involves many twists and turns but it's not possible

13
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:53,440
to get lost as there is only one single path.

14
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:58,800
In modern times the word labyrinth has taken on a different meaning and is often used as

15
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:01,320
a synonym for a maze.

16
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:09,240
A maze is quite different as it is a kind of puzzle with an intricate network of paths.

17
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:14,440
Mases became fashionable in the 15th and 16th centuries in Europe and can still be found

18
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:18,040
in the gardens of great houses and palaces.

19
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:22,720
The paths are usually surrounded by thick high hedges so that it's not possible to

20
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:24,780
see over them.

21
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:30,080
Entering a maze usually involves getting lost a few times before using logic to work out

22
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:35,240
the pattern and find your way to the centre and then out again.

23
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:40,120
There are lots of dead ends and paths which lead you back to where you started.

24
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:46,560
The word maze is believed to come from a Scandinavian word for a state of confusion.

25
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:51,560
This is where the word amazing comes from.

26
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:55,640
Labyrinths on the other hand have a very different function.

27
00:02:55,640 --> 00:03:01,160
Although people now often refer to things they find complicated as labyrinths, this

28
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:04,280
is not how they were seen in the past.

29
00:03:04,280 --> 00:03:09,920
The winding spiral of the labyrinth has been used for centuries as a metaphor for life's

30
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:11,520
journey.

31
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:17,200
It served as a spiritual reminder that there is purpose and meaning to our lives and helped

32
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:20,600
to give people a sense of direction.

33
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:25,400
Labyrinths are thought to encourage a feeling of calm and have been used as a meditation

34
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:30,400
and prayer tool in many cultures over many centuries.

35
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:35,680
The earliest examples of the labyrinth spiral pattern have been found carved into stone

36
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:42,520
from Sardinia to Scandinavia, from Arizona to India to Africa.

37
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:47,200
In Europe these spiral carvings date from the late Bronze Age.

38
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:53,640
The Native American Pima tribe wove baskets with a circular labyrinth design that depicted

39
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:56,120
their own cosmology.

40
00:03:56,120 --> 00:04:03,720
In ancient Greece the labyrinth spiral was used on coins around 4000 years ago.

41
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:09,120
Labyrinths made of mosaics were commonly found in bathhouses, villas and tombs throughout

42
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:11,760
the Roman Empire.

43
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:17,800
In Northern Europe there were actual physical labyrinths designed for walking on.

44
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:22,680
These were cut into the turf or grass, usually in a circular pattern.

45
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:27,960
The origin of these walking labyrinths remains unclear but they were probably used for fertility

46
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:31,960
rites which may date back thousands of years.

47
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:37,780
Eleven examples of turf labyrinths survive today, including the largest one at Saffron

48
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:47,280
Walden, England, which used to have a large tree in the middle of it.

49
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:51,460
More recently labyrinths have experienced something of a revival.

50
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:56,140
Some believe that walking a labyrinth promotes healing and mindfulness and there are those

51
00:04:56,140 --> 00:05:01,620
who believe in its emotional and physical benefits which include slower breathing and

52
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:05,260
a restored sense of balance and perspective.

53
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:10,860
This idea has become so popular that labyrinths have been laid into the floors of spars, wellness

54
00:05:10,860 --> 00:05:16,180
centres and even prisons in recent years.

55
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:21,940
A pamphlet at Colorado Children's Hospital informs patients that walking a labyrinth can

56
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:26,060
often calm people in the midst of a crisis.

57
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:29,700
And apparently it's not only patients who benefit.

58
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:34,580
Many visitors find walking a labyrinth less stressful than sitting in a corridor or waiting

59
00:05:34,580 --> 00:05:35,980
room.

60
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:39,940
Some doctors even walk the labyrinth during their breaks.

61
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:45,180
In some hospitals patients who can't walk can have a paper finger labyrinth brought

62
00:05:45,180 --> 00:05:46,820
to their bed.

63
00:05:46,820 --> 00:05:51,280
The science behind the theory is a little sketchy but there are dozens of small scale

64
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:55,420
studies which support claims about the benefits of labyrinths.

65
00:05:55,420 --> 00:06:01,540
For example, one study found that walking a labyrinth provided short term calming, relaxation

66
00:06:01,540 --> 00:06:06,100
and relief from anxiety for Alzheimer's patients.

67
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:14,940
So what is it about labyrinths that makes their appeal so universal?

68
